  "slug": "plasma-membrane-er-52a1",
  "summary": "New work resolves how E-Syt2 and Pil1 phosphorylation and FRM condensates reorganize ER-plasma membrane interfaces, alongside cytoskeletal patterning cues.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is regulated organization of the cell cortex and ER-plasma membrane interfaces. In mitosis, phosphorylation of E-Syt2 drives loss of ERPM contact sites, and retaining them delays cell progression. In fission yeast, Pdk1 phosphorylates Pil1 to disassemble eisosomes and remodel plasma membrane and cortical ER at the division site, enabling Mid1 recruitment. Complementary preprints show that FRM-4 and FRM-1 phase separation partitions ER-plasma membrane junctions into calcium-release and calcium-reentry subdomains, and that a specialized extracellular matrix patterns the microtubule regulator EFA-6 at neuron-epidermis interfaces. The MTCL1/2 study extends the cytoskeletal theme to the Golgi region, where katanin and CAPZ recruitment coordinates microtubules and actin for secretory vesicle docking. New here is the mechanistic resolution of phosphorylation and condensate-based control at membrane contacts and cortex.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
